  "Question": "What is the units digit of \\(19^{19} + 99^{99}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(8\\)",
    "E": "\\(9\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The units digit of a product depends only on the units digits of its factors: every contribution involving a tens place is a multiple of \\(10\\).\n\nBoth bases end in \\(9\\). Since \\(9\\cdot9=81\\) ends in \\(1\\), while \\(1\\cdot9=9\\) ends in \\(9\\), successive positive powers of either base have units digits\n\\[\n9,1,9,1,\\ldots.\n\\]\nThus an odd power ends in \\(9\\). Both \\(19\\) and \\(99\\) are odd, so the two terms each end in \\(9\\). Their units digits add to \\(9+9=18\\), making the units digit of the sum \\(\\boxed{8}\\), choice D.",
